{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Only his rest shall he give, and healing shall he heal.</b> Meaning: even after he has risen and walks about outside, if he is still unable to work and cannot do his labor, or if he still needs a physician for the remnants of the illness resulting from the wound — this he is obligated to pay." "And the verse's saying 'only' may be explained in light of what Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Hovel uMazzik 2): they estimate the cost of healing, etc., and likewise the idleness they appraise, etc., and if, etc., and if the illness lasted longer than what they appraised, he adds nothing to him, etc. — in what case is this said? When the one who caused the damage wanted this arrangement for himself, etc., end quote. Therefore the verse says 'only,' etc. — meaning, to exclude: there is a scenario in which he does not pay his rest and healing, namely if he preempted and fixed a sum, etc.; but without a fixed stipulation, he pays until he is completely healed." "<b>And healing shall he heal.</b> This may be explained in light of what Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Hovel uMazzik 2:14): if the injured party said to him, 'Fix a sum,' etc., 'and I will heal myself' — they do not listen to him. And this is why the verse said <b>ורפא ירפא</b> and did not say 'he shall give,' as it says regarding the preceding word 'his rest' — where it should have said 'his rest and his healing he shall give.' And many derashot have been said regarding this doubling."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:55.818242261Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}